{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>For every matter of negligence, etc.</b> They said, their memory for blessing (Bava Metzia 57b): ״עַל כָּל״ etc. — a general rule, ״עַל שׁוֹר״ etc. — a specification, ״עַל כָּל״ etc. — it returned and generalized. Just as the specification — something movable whose body is money, etc. — thus excluding landed property, which is not movable, and slaves, which are likened to landed property; excluding documents, whose body is not money. Thus far. And Rambam ruled accordingly in chapter 2 of the Laws of Theft. Now, the plain sense of the verses indicates that even though an unpaid bailee who was negligent, etc., is exempt — for absent negligence, he is exempt even for money and utensils." "And I saw that Rambam wrote in chapter 2 of the Laws of Hiring, and this is his language: ״It appears to me that if the bailee was negligent regarding slaves and similar things, he is liable to pay, for he is exempt regarding slaves, landed property, and documents only from the law of theft and loss — that if he was an unpaid bailee and they were stolen or lost, etc., he is exempt from oath, etc. But if he was negligent with them he is liable to pay, for every negligent party is a damager, and there is no difference between the law of one who damages land and the law of one who damages movables. And this is a true law for those who understand,״ etc. Thus far. And the Ravs, their memory for blessing, disputed him with a plausible argument, namely that if a negligent party is truly a damager, he would not be exempt when the owner is with him, for the verse says ״לֹא יְשַׁלֵּם״ — and even if he was negligent — as Rambam himself wrote in chapter 1 of the Laws of Hiring, and this is his language: ״And even if he was negligent, etc., and it was lost due to the negligence, he is exempt.״ Thus far. And it is obvious that the law of a negligent party is not like the law of a damager, for the negligent party is neither a guardian nor a damager, but rather through the withdrawal of his guardianship the damage came on its own; whereas the damager himself causes the damage. And they further objected: if so, an unpaid bailee would in any case be liable to take an oath that he was not negligent, since if he had been negligent he would be liable." "<b>And it appears</b> to say that what they objected from guardianship in the owner's presence is different, for regarding the owner's presence the verse explicitly revealed that he is exempt even though he caused damage, as is understood from the verses and as they expounded regarding the borrower. But regarding what the verse excluded landed property and documents from the law of an unpaid bailee, we do not find that it excluded them explicitly even for negligence, for negligence was not mentioned at all — only unauthorized use (shelikhut yad), as it is written ״אִם לֹא שָׁלַח״. And what we hold an unpaid bailee liable for regarding negligence is only from the aspect of the law of a damager — so how can we apply the exclusion to a matter that was not mentioned at all? And if you say ״דְּבַר פֶּשַׁע,״ they have already explained it to refer to negligence regarding an instance where he himself stole it and claims it was stolen; but the matter of negligence was not mentioned. And it is true that if negligence had no aspect of the law of a damager, we would say that the verse excluded it from all laws of an unpaid bailee even though it was not mentioned. However, since we see that what our Sages, their memory for blessing, held a negligent party liable for is from the law of a damager — if so, whence do we have grounds to be lenient in the matter? And therefore Rambam wrote that there are no grounds to exempt him." "And if you say: since a negligent party has the status of a damager, if so a homeowner who ignited his hired worker's grain heap or garment would be exempt, for he is a negligent damager and the Rakhmana exempted him. And perhaps the verse was lenient only regarding a bailee. Further, it appears that even Rambam would concede that he does not have the status of a damager in the full sense, and if he burned the garment deposited with him from the owners he is liable for it — and he is like a damager but not a damager in the full sense. And you will find that Rambam wrote in chapter 21 of the Laws of Marriage: ״A woman who broke vessels during her work is exempt from rabbinic restitution,״ thus far. This shows that breaking by hand is exempt only by rabbinic enactment. And as for what they objected against him — if so, he would be liable to take an oath for negligence — Rambam wrote in chapter 4 of the Laws of Borrowing and Deposit: ״One who deposits with another without payment and it was stolen, etc. — behold this one swears, etc., if he did not lay his hand, etc., and they roll the oath upon him that he was not negligent,״ thus far. It is implied from the words of the Rav that the principal oath by Torah law is only when it was stolen and is not in his possession; but the oath that he was not negligent we arrive at by way of gilgul (rolling). And from this it follows that the oath of 'he was not negligent' mentioned regarding an unpaid bailee is a rabbinic enactment, for the Torah said only that he did not lay his hand. And therefore, whenever the Torah oath applies, the Sages rolled onto it the oath of 'not negligent'; and whenever the Torah oath does not apply and exempts him, the gilgul is void." "<b>We thus find ourselves saying</b> that the exclusion of the deposits — landed property, etc. — applies only to the oath of an unpaid bailee that he did not lay his hand, specifically. And if they claim he was negligent with it, he would be liable to take an oath of heseikh (establishing silence/non-response) according to the enactment of Rav Naḥman (Bava Metzia 5a). And according to this, what they said at the end of the chapter הַחוֹבֵל (Bava Kamma 93a) regarding that charity purse that Rav Yosef deposited with that man — it was stolen and Rav Yosef held him liable, and Abayye objected: ״to guard and not to distribute״ — if so, that he holds the verse excludes even from the law of a negligent party, for there the negligence was the basis for holding him liable, and he was an unpaid bailee. According to what I wrote on the verse ״לִשְׁמֹר,״ this law — that he is liable for distributing — is by reasoning exempt without the exclusion derived from לִשְׁמֹר, but they anchored it to the verse, or the verse came to prevent error in His words, so as to hold liable even for distributing. Therefore the plausible reasoning is clear to you, that any money that has no claimants is exempt, for he may say to each person, etc. — which is not so regarding the law of negligence concerning money that has claimants, where reason dictates that a negligent party is a damager — unless there is a clear proof to exempt him, as explained; when the owner is not present, the law stands as it is."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {4 "In comment [4], I read שֶׁלֹּא בִּבְעָלִים (״when the owner is not present״) against what might be expected contextually; the phrase appears to resume the discussion from comments [2]–[3] about whether the exemption is limited to the owner's presence. The Aramaic legal terms מִעוּטָא (exclusion) and הֶסֵּת (heseikh, a procedural oath) are retained in transliteration."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:53.104508427Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}